{
  "id": "grammar.a2.12.03.preguntas",
  "title": "Questions with Direct Object Pronouns",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "Ask what someone bought, gave or received with questions such as ¿*Qué* *compraste*? and answer by using a direct object pronoun when the object is already understood: Lo *compré*. ¿Qué *recibiste*? La *recibí*. The pronoun comes before the conjugated verb."
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "spanish": "¿Qué compraste?",
          "english": "What did you buy?"
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Lo compré.",
          "english": "I bought it."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "¿Qué recibiste?",
          "english": "What did you receive?"
        },
        {
          "spanish": "La recibí.",
          "english": "I received it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        [
          "¿Qué compraste?",
          "What did you buy?"
        ],
        [
          "¿Qué recibiste?",
          "What did you receive?"
        ],
        [
          "Lo compré.",
          "I bought it."
        ],
        [
          "La recibí.",
          "I received it."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "tip",
      "content": "Repeating the noun in your answer (*Compré el regalo*) is grammatical but sounds stiff — once the object is already on the table, a Spanish speaker reaches for the pronoun instead: *lo compré*."
    },
    {
      "type": "external-link",
      "topic": "direct object pronouns",
